{"name":"Cosy Cottage","hex":"#f2ddd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cosy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Affording comfort and warmth; snug; social and comfortable.","Warm and comfortable."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A padded or knit covering put on an item to keep it warm, especially a teapot or egg.","A padded or knit covering for any item (often an electronic device such as a laptop computer)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cosy"},{"term":"cottage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small house.","A seasonal home of any size or stature, a recreational home or a home in a remote location."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stay at a seasonal home, to go cottaging.","To have homosexual sex in a public lavatory; to practice cottaging."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cottage"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cottage","blocks":[{"text":"A cottage during England's feudal period was the holding by a cottager of a small house with enough garden to feed a family and in return for the cottage, the cottager had to provide some form of service to the manorial lord. However, in time cottage just became the general term for a small house. In modern usage, a cottage is usually a modest, often cosy dwelling, typically in a rural or semi-rural location and not necessarily in England. The cottage orné, often quite large and grand residences built by the nobility, dates back to a movement of \"rustic\" stylised cottages of the late 18th and early 19th century during the Romantic movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Country_cottage%2C_Inch_Island_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3951065_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottage"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia